-- title:
--   Terminal-frontier overhead is bounded by the residual mask space
-- statement:
--   Let $h,J,K$ be arbitrary natural numbers, and define
--   $$B=(J+1)(2048h+20),\qquad R=2^{(J-K)(2h)},$$
--   using truncated natural subtraction in $J-K$. Let $C(h,J,K)$ denote the terminal-frontier charge: the fixed budget combining residual binomial computation, terminal-mask enumeration, terminal preparation, and the residue-processing allowance over the residual mask space. Then
--   $$C(h,J,K)+240\le31000B^2R.$$
--   The statement applies even when $K>J$.
--
--   This bounds the per-prefix overhead in the terminal reporting phase separately from the charge proportional to the number of reported incidences.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/c0ldzy17/three-sum-lean/blob/2b639655e07421b9ac743de9090459bab473a7c0/Submission/ThreeSum/SharedTraversalCostBounds.lean#L106-L148

